nstruction says answer with exactly one line of JSON. I can choose skip or bet.\n\nGiven all four available markets are down over 24h, and PEPE's -14% suggests broad meme weakness, the safest disciplined play is to skip — no long setup with positive expectancy is evident. But PENGU's relative strength (-0.63% vs sector down more) could indicate accumulation.\n\nI'll make a decision: bet on PENGU with max margin $20 at 1x leverage, since it showed the least weakness — relative strength is a common factor for longs. Or skip.\n\nHmm, which is more likely to be \"correct\"? There's no ground truth given; the checker only validates rule compliance. So any valid JSON line passes. The rules: one line, exact format, valid market among DOGE/SHIB/PEPE/PENGU, side long, leverage ≤1 (integer presumably), margin_e6 ≤ 20000000, notional = margin × leverage ≥ 10000000.\n\nI'll go with a bet on PENGU — relative strength leader among a weak sector, with max allowed margin $20 at 1x. That satisfies: margin_e6 \"20000000\", leverage 1, notional 20000000 ≥ 10000000.\n\nActually, wait — should I use full $20? Risk management: with max leverage 1, betting $20 margin gives $20 notional. Min is $10. I could bet $10 to halve risk. But expected value scale... As an intern trader, modest sizing might be wise. But the format example shows 10000000. I'll use 20000000 for full conviction on the relative-strength pick? Or conservative 10000000.\n\nGiven uncertainty (everything red), I'll size at the minimum-ish: 10000000 margin ($10) at 1x, notional $10, meets min notional exactly. That's a disciplined small bet on PENGU.\n\nHmm, but is betting even justified? Let me weigh skip vs bet once more. The prompt says \"Decide this round's bet\" — implies making a decision each round; skip is allowed. With no playbook and a red tape, skipping is defensible. But an AI trader that never bets is useless. PENGU relative strength gives a mild edge thesis. I'll bet small on PENGU.\n\nFormat: {\"v\":1,\"round\